{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the boundary-objects note at head 99890d0497528133432fa2d2f51ca4b1cad7dda9, tree 25278dc35fb1a1043c0924a70a207901c8347b96, for request bc4aa093 and review request b7e0010a. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds three commits to main 2be6ca74 and changes one file, `notes/2026-10-28-boundary-objects-in-use.md`. I read the artifact at the head first, then the diff from a9d65044, against the four items and four points of report 055be7a9. The diff changes nothing else. For the rest of the note, the findings of 055be7a9 stand: the one-act design carries every case in sections 3 and 4 and the three rules of section 5, and the \"What changes where\" table matches main 2be6ca74.\n\n## Items of 055be7a9\n\n1. Witnesses for BO2 and BO5: resolved.\n   - In W2, B writes a mark from B's view. The host shows the exact act, signs it on B's action alone, and the shared room shows the mark with B as author.\n   - B's view then offers a note in A's own room. The host refuses it because B reads no scope there, and the view shows the refusal. That tests the boundary of what a view may write.\n   - W5 reruns W2 with B's view as code in the sandbox domain. The code reaches only the sources that its `about` names, and the host shows and signs every act on B's action.\n   - BO2 names W2 with the mark written from the view, and BO5 names W5.\n2. Fragment standards: resolved. The note now says that the media type defines what a fragment means, as RFC 3986 says. It cites the W3C Media Fragments URI for `t` and `xywh`, RFC 8118 for `page` in PDF, and RFC 7111 for `row` in CSV. The Source line says the same.\n3. Comparisons: resolved. The Xanadu sentence and the block-based tools sentence are out of the body. The Source keeps both as citations.\n4. The matter: resolved. A closing is about the package item's opening entry when there is one, and otherwise about the terms note or the reading. The notes table, the closings paragraph and W4 all say so. The matter view shows each party's last word about that entry beside the item's state.\n\n## Points of 055be7a9\n\n- Point 1: resolved. A note is a revision only when its text is data or a declaration. A prose note about one's own prose note is a follow-up unless its text says it is a revision.\n- Point 2: resolved. The host names the glyphs it reads as agreement, and a view shows every other glyph as a reaction.\n- Point 3: resolved. An older room refuses a commons package with `commons-missing`.\n- Point 4: resolved. Section 3.2 is rewrapped.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 2be6ca74 gives the head tree 25278dc3.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 738 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 2be6ca74: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages from 2be6ca74: 3 checked, 0 flagged. The longest subject has 62 characters.\n- `git diff --check`: clean. No prose line is over 79 characters.\n- The body names no product and makes no comparison.\n\n## A point that does not block\n\nThe \"a revision\" row of the notes table still reads \"the same media type as an earlier note by the same signer\". The paragraph below it now limits revisions to data and declarations. Give the row the same limit, so that a reader who reads only the table gets the rule.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe commons is one native definition with one act, no guards and no effects. Threads, revisions, views, agreements and closings are readings by the host. Binding stays in packages, on the `from: { signer: true }` effect that the contract has today. Each slice now has a witness, and the matter view has a reference to read. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\n- A party binds only by its own signature on a package act that names the terms. W3 proves that a glyph fills no slot.\n- A view writes only acts that its member may sign, shown in full and signed only on the member's action. W2 proves the refusal of a write in a room the member does not read, and W5 holds code views to the same rule and to their named sources.\n- A view in a shared room names a private note by fact reference, never by text digest, and shows an unreadable source with its room named.\n\nApproved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe design is one act and one string on a reference. The diff adds one witness row and the clauses that the four items needed, and it removes two sentences. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - the one-act design carries every case in sections 3 and 4 and the three rules of section 5;\n  - W1 to W5 prove what BO1 to BO5 need;\n  - the table matches main 2be6ca74;\n  - sections 5, 8 and 9 meet the AGENTS.md writing rule.\n\nThe landing check will follow the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fa2572f679a16984fd228dde8ed51ea8ba092e70","binding":"assigned","head":"99890d0497528133432fa2d2f51ca4b1cad7dda9","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e1532509185786e8e063307aedbed5b7d46b2cad\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0072639d674d37e8b6686dcf7d43b9c625363c44\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e1532509185786e8e063307aedbed5b7d46b2cad","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}